# Build-time env for the production frontend bundle. TRACKED on purpose --
# frontend/.gitignore has an explicit `!.env.production`, because the Vercel
# project reads this file at build time and a missing file silently drops the
# backend URL.
#
# None of this is a secret. VITE_-prefixed values are inlined into the browser
# bundle and are readable by anyone who loads the page. Real secrets
# (DATABASE_URL, GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET, OPENAI_API_KEY) live only in Render.
#
# STALENESS IS THE RISK HERE, not leakage. These values are a point-in-time
# copy of a deploy that someone made by hand, so they go out of date the moment
# a backend or a domain changes. Precedence:
#
#   Vercel dashboard env vars  ->  win
#   .env.local                 ->  wins locally
#   this file                  ->  the fallback
#   src/lib/config.ts          ->  the dev fallback
#
# When you redeploy a backend or add a domain, update the Vercel dashboard
# first, then this file, so the two cannot disagree. See docs/MANUAL_STEPS.md.
VITE_API_URL=https://codeatlas-qr0e.onrender.com
VITE_USE_MOCKS=false

# Intentionally EMPTY, and it must stay that way.
#
# The previous deploys hardcoded a Vercel hostname here, and every one of them
# went stale -- the repo's docs carried two different dead hostnames for months,
# and the deployed bundle pointed at whichever one was written last. Canonicals,
# og:url and sitemap.xml all read from this value, so a wrong one produces
# split-canonical URLs that only fail once a crawler has already indexed them.
#
# Leave it to the Vercel dashboard, where the current origin is a deliberate
# act rather than a copy of something old. The build warns loudly when this is
# unset (see vite.config.ts `seoFiles`), so the failure is visible at deploy
# time rather than in Search Console a month later.
VITE_SITE_URL=